  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSevere Thunderstorm Watch #687 has been canceled for Ulster, \nDutchess and Litchfield Counties. The threat for thunderstorms\nhas diminished with the front moving across western New England\nearly tonight.  In the wake of the cold front brisk/blustery \nconditions occur, as west/northwest winds 10-20 mph shift to the\nnorthwest with the cold advection. Some gusts 25-35 mph will \nlikely occur with a few isolated gusts around 40 mph funneling \ndown the Mohawk Valley into the Capital Region, northern \nTaconics and the Berkshires. Some gusty winds are also possible \nover the northern/eastern Catskills. In the wake of the front \nwith the mid and upper trough approaching, some upslope rain to \nsnow showers will occur over the western Dacks and southern \nGreens. Some light snow accums of a coating to a few tenths of \ninch may occur. Some light snow showers/flurries could occur \nover the northern Catskills, northern Taconics and Berkshires \ndue to upslope and lake enhanced showers. The low- level flow \nquickly veers to the northwest with some multi-bands of lake \neffect potentially grazing the northern and eastern Catskills \nSaturday morning. Latest NAEFS indicate H850 temp anomalies will\nbe 1 to 2 STDEVs below normal. The actual H850 temps fall to \n-6C to -9C from Albany north and west into the Adirondacks to 0C\nto -6C readings south and east towards the I-84 corridor by \ndaybreak Saturday. Temps will fall quickly with widespread lows \nin the 30s to lower 40s with some upper 20s over the \nsouth/southwest Adirondacks and southern Greens. Wind chill \ntemps or \"feels-like\" temps when combining the winds with the \nair temps will be in the teens to mid/upper 20s over the higher \nterrain with mainly 30s in the valleys with some mid/upper 20s \nin the western Mohawk Valley. It will feel like early Winter. \n\nUpslope and lake effect snow showers/flurries or sprinkles will end \nin the late morning with the subsidence inversion lowering. The\nbrisk northwest winds will continue with chilly temps with \ndaytime highs only in the 40s in the valleys with a few readings\nnear 50F in the Hudson Valley and 30s to lower 40s over the \nhills and mtns. The winds will persist from the northwest at \n10-20 mph with some gusts 25-35 mph due to the sfc pressure \ngradient and some deeper mixing. We increased gusts slightly \nblending in the latest NBM 90th percentile. The latest NBM 24-hr\nprobs for gusts >35 mph are low from 2 am Sat to 2 am Sun, as \nthey are only 20-40% over the northern Taconics/northern \nBerkshires and eastern Catskills. Gusts > 40 mph are less than \n15%, so no Wind Advisories expected. Wind Chills/\"Feels-like\" \ntemps will continue in the 20s and 30s for much of the day. The \nwinds subside fairly quickly Saturday night with the sfc high \nbuilding in from the Ohio Valley over PA and NY. Lows fall into\nthe 20s to around 30F. The growing season is over, but a \nwidespread frost will occur across the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will be overhead and nearby NY and New England to\nclose the weekend with temps rebounding after a cold start into\nthe mid/upper 40s to mid 50s across eastern NY and western New \nEngland with mostly sunny skies and light winds. Another chilly \nnight is expected Sunday night with lows in the mid 20s to mid \n30s, but temps moderate to seasonal levels in the 50s to around \n60F on Monday with partly to mostly sunny skies. Dry weather and\nseasonable temps continue through the mid week, as we monitor a\npotential nor'easter near the Mid Atlantic Coast during the mid\nweek (Tue- Wed time frame).",
